# 24. Suggested Post-Draft Review Prompt

Use this after generating a first draft:

> Review this article using the GPL Technologies Editorial Review Layer, GPL Brand Writing Style Guide, relevant Business Context, and specific article assignment.
>
> Begin with diagnosis rather than line editing. Determine whether the draft has a strong central argument or whether it reads like an assignment expanded into paragraphs.
>
> Assume the reader is technically informed.
>
> Apply strict editorial compression. Do not preserve material for word count, assignment completeness, AEO terminology, introductory buildup, generic production context, or because the article feels short.
>
> Remove anything that fails the “yes, obviously” test unless it is necessary to support the next inference.
>
> Preserve and strengthen connected reasoning so sentences carry cause, sequence, dependency, qualification, and consequence. Avoid short statement stacking, claim + list + consequence structure, lists disguised as prose, excessive examples, hand-holding, TED Talk-style lines, staged insight, and unnecessary contrast framing.
>
> Make sure every section adds a distinct layer to the argument. Combine or remove sections when they repeat the same idea.
>
> Treat the article assignment as a guide to the subject, research boundaries, and exclusions rather than a requirement to turn every assignment bullet into prose.
>
> Keep GPL positioning restrained and business-unit appropriate.
>
> A shorter article is not incomplete if the argument is complete.
>
> If a section is fundamentally weak, rebuild it rather than lightly editing it.

---

# 25. Final Editorial Rule

If a sentence sounds like it is trying to sound insightful, state the idea plainly.

If a paragraph feels like claim + list + consequence, rebuild it around the underlying logic.

If several sentences are fragments of the same thought, connect the reasoning.

If a section proves the same point another section already established, cut or consolidate it.

If a paragraph explains something the informed reader already knows, remove it.

If a paragraph exists because the assignment mentioned the subject, decide whether the argument actually needs it.

If a draft is polished but not useful, stronger prose will not solve the problem. Strengthen the idea.

If the writing sounds like a template, rebuild the paragraph around how the system, workflow, risk, or dependency actually behaves.

If the article is shorter after editing, that is not a problem.

A successful GPL article should feel calm, clear, technically informed, production-aware, and human.

It should respect the reader's intelligence and use connected reasoning so the prose feels like one thought naturally leading into the next rather than separate statements assembled on the page.

It should demonstrate expertise through judgment rather than volume.

**If the argument is complete, stop writing.**
